{"packet_id":"D040-01","doc_id":"D040","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Los Angeles Just Cause for Eviction Ordinance (JCO)","requirement":"A landlord may not end a tenancy without just cause and must pay relocation assistance for no-fault evictions. For non-payment, eviction is barred unless the rent owed exceeds the unit's Fair Market Rent; eviction notices must be filed with LAHD within three business days.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Most City of Los Angeles residential properties not regulated by the RSO; can apply to buildings newer than October 1, 1978 and to single-family dwellings; tenant must have lived in the unit six months or their lease expired.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"Does not cover properties regulated by the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO)"},{"type":"other","text":"Can apply to buildings newer than October 1, 1978 and to a property with only one single-family dwelling"},{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"some properties owned by HACLA or the government","unit_limit":null}],"per_tenancy":"Applies to a tenancy once the tenant has lived in the same unit at least six months or their original lease expired, whichever comes first; at-fault evictions get no relocation assistance.","coverage_quotes":["The JCO covers most residential properties in the City of Los Angeles that are not regulated by the City’s Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO).","In order to apply to a tenancy, it requires that the tenant either has lived in the same unit for at least six months or that their original lease expired, whichever comes first.","There are other specific exceptions to the JCO including, but not limited to, transient hotels, licensed care facilities, fraternity or sorority houses, owner’s roommate, cooperatives under certain circumstances, some non-profit facilities for the homeless or short-term treatment centers for substance abuse, and some properties owned by HACLA or the government."]},"exemptions":"Transient hotels, licensed care facilities, fraternity or sorority houses, owner's roommate, cooperatives under certain circumstances, some non-profit facilities for the homeless or short-term treatment centers for substance abuse, and some HACLA- or government-owned properties.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Los Angeles Just Cause Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §165.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"It prohibits terminations of tenancies without just cause and requires relocation assistance for no-fault evictions.","interaction":"Single-family homes and condominiums exempt from state law are not exempt from the JCO.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D040-01","doc_id":"D040","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California Tenant Protection Act of 2019 (AB 1482) rent cap","requirement":"Annual rent increases for covered units are capped at 5% plus the regional cost-of-living change, or 10%, whichever is lower; the maximum allowable increase was 8.9% from August 1, 2024 to July 31, 2025.","key_value":"5% + CPI, max 10%; 8.9% for Aug 1, 2024 – Jul 31, 2025","coverage_conditions":"Buildings older than 15 years; excludes units constructed within the last 15 years, units already subject to the City's RSO, deed-restricted affordable units, and qualifying single-family homes and condominiums.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":15,"basis":"construction"},{"type":"units","role":"exempt","op":"at_most","n":2},{"type":"other","text":"Units restricted by a deed, regulatory restriction or recorded document limiting affordability to low or moderate-income households"},{"type":"other","text":"Units already subject to the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO)"},{"type":"other","text":"Single-family homes and condominiums exempt only if not owned by a real estate trust, corporation or LLC with a corporate member and the landlord gave written notice"}],"per_tenancy":"A two-unit property is exempt only if an owner occupied the second unit for the entire tenancy; the single-family or condominium exemption also requires the landlord's written notice under Civil Code Sections 1946.2(e)(8)(B)(i) and 1947.12(d)(5)(B)(i).","coverage_quotes":["-Units that were constructed within the last 15 years (this applies on a rolling basis – i.e.. a unit constructed on January 1, 2006 is not covered as of January, 1 2020, but is covered on and after January 1, 2021).","-A two-unit property, provided the second unit was occupied by an owner of the property for the entire period of the tenancy."]},"exemptions":"Units constructed within the last 15 years; units restricted by a deed or recorded document limiting affordability to low- or moderate-income households; certain dormitories; an owner-occupied two-unit property; qualifying single-family homes and condominiums; units already subject to the City's RSO.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2024-08-01","valid_through":"2025-07-31","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.12","quoted_span":"Effective August 1, 2024 to July 31, 2025, the maximum allowable increase is 8.9%. Previously, from August 1, 2023 to July 31, 2024 the maximum annual increase for units subject to AB 1482 was 8.8%.","interaction":"Units already subject to the City's Rent Stabilization Ordinance are not covered by this state law.","relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
